# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Resource {
  static func create(parent: borrowing Handle, kind: ResourceKind,
                     base: UInt64, size: UInt64, name: String,
                     exclusive: Bool = false) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func info(_ resource: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)
    -> ResourceInfo
}

struct ResourceInfo: Equatable, Sendable {
  var kind: UInt32
  var flags: UInt32
  var base: UInt64
  var size: UInt64
  var name: String
  init(kind: UInt32, flags: UInt32, base: UInt64, size: UInt64,
       name: String)
}

enum IOPorts {
  static func request(_ resource: borrowing Handle, base: UInt16,
                      count: UInt16) throws(Status)
  static func release(_ resource: borrowing Handle, base: UInt16,
                      count: UInt16) throws(Status)
  static func read(_ port: UInt16, width: Int) -> UInt32
  static func write(_ port: UInt16, width: Int, _ value: UInt32)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A resource is the right to a range of hardware: MMIO addresses, interrupts, I/O ports, or a system capability.
A child resource lies inside its parent's range.
The launcher hands each service the ranged roots its manifest grants; see manifest(6).

`create` makes a resource of `kind` covering `size` from `base` inside `parent`'s range, with `name`.
With `exclusive`, no other resource may overlap it; this works only from a ranged root.
It is `resource_create(2)`.

`info` returns the resource's `kind` and `flags`, its `base`, its `size` (0 for a kind's ranged root) and its `name`.

`IOPorts` is for amd64.
A process may use the I/O ports it has requested, with an I/O port resource that covers them; any other port access faults.
`request` and `release` take `count` ports from `base` (`ioports_request(2)`, `ioports_release(2)`).
`read` executes `in` and `write` executes `out`, of `width` 1, 2 or 4 bytes.

